---
export const prerender = true;

import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/header/nav.astro";
import Prompt from "../../components/Prompt.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import talkData from "../../data/talks.json";

const talk = talkData[0];

const SLIDES = talk.links[0].url;
const REPO = talk.links[1].url;

const links = [
  { title: "Slides", url: SLIDES },
  { title: "Demo source", url: REPO },
];

// The three primitives, as the talk laid them out: what it is, what it costs.
const primitives = [
  {
    name: "Tools",
    kind: "verb",
    example: "get_speaker(handle)",
    body: "Actions the agent decides to invoke. The model reasons about whether to call one, then calls it.",
    cost: "Side effects, plus latency and tokens on every call — and the set you expose is the set that client is allowed to do.",
    metaphor: "Ordering the dish.",
  },
  {
    name: "Resources",
    kind: "noun",
    example: "meetup://events/next",
    body: "Context read by URI, with no “should I call this?” reasoning step in front of it.",
    cost: "Cheap, cacheable context — a URI is a cache key, an action is not.",
    metaphor: "The menu.",
  },
  {
    name: "Prompts",
    kind: "template",
    example: "draft_rsvp_reminder",
    body: "Parameterised prompts the server ships for its own domain, invoked explicitly rather than triggered.",
    cost: "Domain phrasing owned by the server that owns the domain, instead of duplicated across every client.",
    metaphor: "The waiter's recommended pairing.",
  },
];

// One server, two grants. The struck-through tools are the whole argument.
const TOOLSET = [
  "get_speaker",
  "list_events",
  "rsvp",
  "create_event",
  "enrich_speaker_from_github",
];

const READ_ONLY = ["get_speaker", "list_events"];

const agents = [
  { name: "organizer_agent", scope: "full toolset", allowed: TOOLSET },
  { name: "attendee_agent", scope: "read-only subset", allowed: READ_ONLY },
];

const ogImage = new URL(
  "/speaking/gophers-nigeria-2026/desk.jpg",
  Astro.site ?? Astro.url.origin,
).href;
---

<Base
  title="Building Production-Ready MCP Servers — Talk — Adetoye Anointing"
  description="A talk given to the Nigerian Gophers Meetup on 22 August 2026: tools, resources and prompts are three different cost and trust boundaries, and a production MCP server is the backend engineering you already have."
  image={ogImage}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <header class="mt-12 mb-14">
      <Prompt path="~/speaking" caret={false}>
        <span class="text-fg">cat gophers-nigeria-2026.md</span>
      </Prompt>

      <h1 class="mt-6 text-2xl leading-tight text-accent">
        Building Production-Ready MCP Servers for AI Applications and Agents
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed">
        How Go and Google ADK reshape agentic system design.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        My first talk — given to the Nigerian Gophers Meetup on 22 August 2026,
        over Meet, to a room of experience software engineers. The demo was a Go MCP server
        for running a meetup, built for the meetup it was being presented at.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          ["Nigerian Gophers Meetup", "22 Aug 2026", "Virtual", "Go · MCP · ADK"].map(
            (m) => (
              <span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted border border-line px-2 py-0.5">
                {m}
              </span>
            ),
          )
        }
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 flex items-center flex-wrap gap-5 text-xs">
        {
          links.map((link) => (
            <a
              href={link.url}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity"
            >
              {link.title} ↗
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="mb-16">
      <img
        src="/speaking/gophers-nigeria-2026/desk.jpg"
        alt="A desk lit purple: a monitor showing the meeting with someone else's slide up, and a laptop with my title slide — Building Production-Ready MCP Servers for AI Applications and Agents."
        width="1600"
        height="1295"
        loading="eager"
        class="w-full border border-line"
      />
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Waiting to go on. My title slide is up on the laptop; someone else still
        has the room.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <Section title="The argument">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
        I believe plenty of people in that room had already shipped something agentic.
        Some had done it in n8n — a webhook, an LLM node, an HTTP call, a Slack
        message — and it worked, and it delivered real value. Put that beside a
        Go service: a handler, an agent loop, an <code class="text-accent"
          >http.Get</code
        >, a store. Same picture.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        So the claim wasn't that anyone needed a new skill set. It was that the
        skill set is already there and hasn't been pointed at this yet. Structs.
        A map behind a mutex. An HTTP client that handles its own failure.
        Service boundaries. Least privilege. That is most of what a
        production-grade MCP server is made of — what changes is who calls it,
        and how much you can trust the caller.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The demo was built against the event itself:
        <span class="text-fg">Meetup Ops MCP</span>, an MCP server for running a
        meetup. Speakers, events and RSVPs in Go maps behind a
        <code class="text-accent">sync.RWMutex</code> — guarding them because an
        agent may call several tools at once — plus one real external call, to
        GitHub's public API, enriching a speaker from their handle.
        Unauthenticated, 60 requests an hour, no key to configure. Which mattered:
        it had to run live.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Tools, resources, prompts">
      <p class="mb-8 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Most MCP examples show you tools and stop there. The protocol gives you
        three primitives, and they are not three names for the same idea — they
        are three different cost and trust boundaries.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-8 space-y-7">
        {
          primitives.map((p) => (
            <div class="border-l border-line pl-5">
              <div class="flex items-baseline flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
                <h3 class="text-sm text-fg">{p.name}</h3>
                <span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">
                  {p.kind}
                </span>
                <code class="text-xs text-muted">{p.example}</code>
              </div>

              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{p.body}</p>

              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">{p.cost}</p>

              <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-muted italic">{p.metaphor}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The restaurant is how you remember it. The reason to keep them straight
        is operational: <span class="text-fg"
          >collapse all three into tools</span
        > and every piece of context costs a reasoning step, every client reinvents
        the same phrasing slightly differently, and the permission surface becomes
        simply all of it — because nobody ever drew the line where the boundaries
        already were.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="stdio or streamableHTTP">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <code class="text-accent">stdio</code> runs the server as a subprocess over
        stdin and stdout: zero network config, one machine, one process tree. Good
        for local development, CLI tools, single-binary deployments.
        <code class="text-accent">streamableHTTP</code> makes it a real network service
        — two processes with two lifecycles, reachable from anywhere, with server-sent
        events for streaming back.
      </p>

      <blockquote
        class="mt-5 border-l-2 border-accent pl-5 py-1 text-base leading-relaxed text-fg"
      >
        Will my agent and my tool server ever need to scale, deploy, or fail
        independently?
      </blockquote>

      <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Yes — streamableHTTP from day one. No, one local tool for one user —
        stdio is fine. And the opinion I'd defend in the Q&amp;A:
        <span class="text-fg"
          >test locally on the transport you intend to run in production.</span
        > Developing against stdio and switching at deploy time is where the subtle
        bugs hide, because buffering behaviour, connection lifecycle and error propagation
        all differ between the two.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The payoff for getting it right is that the agent and the MCP server
        become two ordinary services — different repos, different scaling
        policies, different teams — and the contract between them is still just
        MCP.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="One server, many agents">
      <p class="mb-8 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Same server, two agents, two grants — and the second one is built by
        filtering the first, not by writing a second server.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-8 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-8 border-y border-line py-6">
        {
          agents.map((agent) => (
            <div>
              <div class="text-sm text-accent">{agent.name}</div>
              <div class="mt-1 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">
                {agent.scope}
              </div>

              <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2">
                {TOOLSET.map((tool) => {
                  const granted = agent.allowed.includes(tool);
                  return (
                    <li
                      class:list={[
                        "text-xs leading-relaxed",
                        granted ? "text-fg" : "text-muted/50 line-through",
                      ]}
                    >
                      {tool}
                    </li>
                  );
                })}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <span class="text-fg">One MCP server, two blast radii.</span> Nothing about
        the server changed — the attendee agent simply never sees the tools that
        write.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Least privilege applies to agents exactly as it applies to service
        accounts and API keys. The toolset a client sees is the permission
        surface; filtering it is how you grant.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The demo">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <a
          href={REPO}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">Meetup Ops MCP</a
        > is the whole talk in a repo — the store, the one GitHub call, the MCP layer
        of tools, resources and prompts, and two Google ADK agents built on top of
        it, one scoped down.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Dependencies are injected at the command binaries and nothing below
        reaches for a global, which is what lets the tests drive the real
        protocol against a fake GitHub with no network at all. There is also a
        <code class="text-accent">probe</code> binary — a raw MCP client that walks
        every tool, resource and prompt with no model in the loop. That is how you
        find out whether the server is broken or the agent is just being creative.
      </p>

      <pre
        class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto border border-line p-4 text-[10px] sm:text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-muted"># the whole thing, from nothing
<span class="text-fg">git clone {REPO}</span>

# full suite, race detector on — no network, no API key
<span class="text-fg">make test</span>

# streamable HTTP on :8080 — the production shape
<span class="text-fg">make run-http</span></pre>
    </Section>

    <Section title="First time out">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
        This was the first time I had presented to a technical audience, and it
        was virtual — which removes the one signal you actually want, whether
        the room is with you.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        I over-prepared the deck and under-prepared the talking, and it shows in
        the middle third: the transport section is the part I would rewrite
        first, because I explained the trade-off before I had made anyone care
        that there was one. What I would keep is building the demo against the
        event itself, so every abstraction had something concrete underneath it,
        and running the live demo rather than hiding behind a recording of it.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Worth doing badly the first time. <span class="text-fg"
          >Looking for the next one.</span
        >
      </p>
    </Section>

    <footer class="pt-8 border-t border-line flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <Prompt path="~/speaking" caret={true}>
        <span class="text-fg">
          <a href="/speaking" class="hover:text-accent transition-colors">
            cd ..
          </a>
        </span>
      </Prompt>

      <a
        href={SLIDES}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="text-xs text-accent hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity"
      >
        slides ↗
      </a>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Base>
